meta {
  name: ListOmschrijvingEncodedEquals
  type: http
  seq: 20
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/web/artikel?omschrijving=A%3DB&pageSize=5
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.pageSize: eq 5
}

tests {
  test("responds in under 5s - was reproduced at 11.8-19.8s live before the fix", function() {
    expect(res.getResponseTime()).to.be.below(5000);
  });
}

docs {
  Regression test for the reported 11.8-19.8s latency / DoS-shaped bug:
  GET /web/artikel?omschrijving=A%3DB&pageSize=5.

  Root cause (confirmed empirically against the live PASOE instance, see
  Backend/docs or the QueryParamHelper/ArtikelBE class headers):
  Luna.Web.Util.QueryParamHelper was NOT actually the culprit for this
  specific input - "%3D" decodes (once) to a literal "=" in the value,
  and GetQueryValue() already only ever splits a pair on its FIRST "="
  (a param name can never contain "="), so pageSize=5 was always parsed
  correctly and omschrijving was always correctly read as the full "A=B"
  - confirmed via a live debug probe before any fix was applied.

  The actual latency lived one layer down, in
  Luna.BusinessLogic.ArtikelBE:GetArtikelen(): with no artnr/groep filter
  to narrow the scan via an index, and an omschrijving value ("A=B")
  that (correctly) matches zero real rows, the loop scanned the ENTIRE
  artikel table (measured live at ~12.9s for this exact request) looking
  for pageSize+1 matches that never arrived - reachable by any end user
  typing an obscure term into a free-text description filter, not just
  crafted %3D/%26 input. Fixed with a scan-depth cap
  (iMaxRowsScanned, ArtikelBE:GetArtikelen) that gives up and reports
  hasMore=true after a bounded number of rows examined, regardless of
  match count - measured live afterwards at ~0.7-0.8s for this request.

  See ListOmschrijvingEncodedAmpersand.bru for the OTHER reported bug
  against this endpoint (a real QueryParamHelper parsing bug, for
  %26 rather than %3D) - that one is a correctness bug, not a latency
  one.
}